TELNET(1)                      P32W Unix                       TELNET(1)

NAME
       telnet - a terminal on another computer

SYNOPSIS
       telnet host [port]

DESCRIPTION
       telnet connects to host (port 23, the telnet server, unless
       port is given) and joins the terminal to the connection:
       what is typed goes there, and what comes back is shown.

       At first the terminal works a line at a time, as usual: P32W
       echoes and edits the line, and sends it when Return is
       pressed. When the server says it will echo, as a login server
       does, telnet sends each key as it is typed and leaves the
       echoing to the server, until the server stops.

       telnet answers the server's questions about the terminal: its
       type is VT100 and its window 80 by 24. It agrees to suppress
       go-ahead and declines other options.

       ^] closes the connection (in line mode, alone at the start of
       a line). The end of input closes it too.

EXAMPLES
       telnet 10.0.2.2 2323
              a server on the Mac, from the emulator

SEE ALSO
       fetch(1), socket(2), select(2)

BUGS
       No telnet> command prompt; ^] only closes. telnet is not
       encrypted: what is typed, passwords too, crosses the network
       as it is.
